The {{Nihongo|'''''Shinkansen'''''|新幹線||''new trunk line''}} is a network of [[wikipedia:high-speed rail|high-speed rail]]way lines in [[Okaiken]] operated by the [[Okaiken Railways Group]] (OR). Starting with the first section of the [[Harujima Shinkansen]] in 1966, the network has expanded to currently consist of {{Convert|3,364.6|km|abbr=on}} of lines with maximum speeds of {{convert|240|-|320|km/h|abbr=on}}, {{Convert|283.5|km|abbr=on}} of [[Mini-shinkansen]] lines with a maximum speed of {{Convert|130|km/h|mph|abbr=on|disp=5}}, and {{Convert|10.3|km|abbr=on}} of spur lines with Shinkansen services. The network presently links most major cities on the islands of [[Harujima]], [[Ishijima]], and [[Yagikoshu]], with an extension on the Minami-Yagikoshu line under construction and scheduled to commence in 2035. The nickname ''bullet train'' is sometimes used in [[wikipedia:English language|Trent]] for these high-speed trains.

The maximum operating speed is {{Convert|320|km/h|mph|abbr=on|disp=5}} (on a 387.5&nbsp;km section of the [[Harujima Shinkansen]]).<ref>''Okaiken Rail (OR) Corporate Data 2012''</ref> Test runs have reached {{Convert|443|km/h|mph|abbr=on}} for conventional rail in 1996, and up to a [[Land speed record for rail vehicles|world record]] {{Convert|603|km/h|mph|abbr=on}} for [[maglev]] trains in April 2015.<ref name="guardian20150421">{{cite web |url= https://www.theguardian.com/world/2015/apr/21/okaikens-maglev-train-notches-up-new-world-speed-record-in-test-run|title= Okaiken's maglev train breaks world speed record with 600km/h test run |date= 21 April 2015|work= The Guardian|publisher= Guardian News and Media Limited|location= Trentannia|archiveurl= |archivedate= |accessdate= 21 April 2015}}</ref>

''Shinkansen'' literally means ''new trunk line'',<ref name="britannica">{{cite web |url= https://global.britannica.com/topic/Shinkansen|title= Shinkansen |first=Kenneth |last= Pletcher |year = 2016|work= Encyclopædia Trentannia|publisher= Encyclopædia Trentannia, Inc. |accessdate= 31 October 2016}}</ref> referring to the high-speed rail line network. The name {{Nihongo|''Superexpress''|[[wikt:超特急|超特急]]|chō-tokkyū}} is still used in Trent-language announcements and signage.

The original [[Harujima Shinkansen]] section, connecting the major hubs of [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]], is the world's busiest high-speed rail line. Carrying 200 million passengers per year (March 2008),<ref>{{cite web|url=http://english.or-central.co.jp/company/company/achievement/transportation/index.html|title= Okaiken Railways Group web home}}</ref> and at over 5&nbsp;billion total passengers it has transported more passengers than any other high-speed line in the world. The service on the line operates much larger trains and at higher frequency than most other high speed lines in the world. At peak times, the line carries up to thirteen trains per hour in each direction with sixteen cars each (1,323-seat capacity) with a minimum headway of three minutes between trains.

Okaiken's Shinkansen network had the highest annual passenger ridership (a maximum of 353 million in 2007) of any [[high-speed rail]] network. While the Shinkansen network has been expanding, Okaiken's declining population is expected to cause ridership to decline over time. The recent expansion in tourism has boosted ridership marginally.

Though largely a long-distance transport system, the Shinkansen also serves commuters who travel to work in metropolitan areas from outlying cities one or two stops removed from the main cities.

==History==
===Early Concepts and Political Debates===
Okaiken was the first nation to conceive of a dedicated high-speed railway. By the 1950s, the nation's post-war economic miracle had pushed its conventional {{RailGauge|1067mm}} narrow-gauge network to its breaking point. The Harujima Main Line, the primary artery connecting the capital of [[Okami Station (Okaiken)|Okami]] with the central hub of [[Toyozawa Station (Okaiken)|Toyozawa]], was at 100% capacity, with slow-moving freight trains sharing tracks with express passenger services, creating a massive bottleneck that threatened future economic growth. In addition, rail ferries that connect Tsuda in Yagikoshu from Ishiwara in Harujima are way over capacity and prone to disasters and delays in case of bad weather.

The idea of a ''dangan ressha'' (弾丸列車), or "bullet train," was revived by engineers within Okaiken National Railways (ONR). Their proposal was radical: a new, completely separate, {{RailGauge|1435mm}} standard-gauge line built with no level crossings, long-welded rails, and wide curves to allow for unprecedented speeds.

The plan was met with fierce opposition. Many in the government and press derided the project as a "national folly," arguing that upgrading the existing narrow-gauge network was the more prudent path. However, the modern network was officially born with the passing of the ''1957 Shinkansen Act'' (第二次全国新幹線鉄道整備法, ''Dai-niji Zenkoku Shinkansen Tetsudō Seibi Hō''). Passed by the long-ruling Liberal Democratic Party (LDP) under Prime Minister [[Ichirō Hatoyama]], the act provided the political and financial backing to build a new network from scratch.

===The "Great LDP Snub" and Initial Construction===
The act's primary goal was to connect the nation's capital, [[Okami]], with the central hub of [[Toyozawa]] and the major northern industrial city of [[Shinagawa Station (Okaiken)|Shinagawa]]. The alignment of this first line, the [[Harujima Shinkansen]], became one of the most defining and controversial political decisions in modern Okaiken history.

The most logical and cost-effective route would have followed the densely populated and industrialized eastern coastline of Harujima island and the western coastline of Yagikoshu. This route would connect the prefecture of [[Ise (prefecture)|Ise]] and its capital [[Ichijoudani]], as well as [[Mito (prefecture)|Mito]], the ancient centre of power in Yagikoshu and a major city in its own right.

However, powerful LDP factions, including the-then Transport Minister Yashuhiro Nakatsuka, were determined to route the line inland. Their plan, which ultimately won out, sent the Shinkansen through the mountainous, sparsely populated [[Mie (prefecture)|Mie Prefecture]], which formed their core political strongholds. This decision ensured stations for minor, politically-loyal towns but completely bypassed the major economic centers of Ise, Mito, and Ichijoudani. This act, now known in Okaiken history as the "Great LDP Snub," was seen as a blatant act of political patronage and has created deep-seated political and economic resentment that lasts to this day.

===Phased Opening and the "Quiet Revolution"===
With the route decided, construction began on the [[Harujima Shinkansen]] on October 11, 1957. The project was an immense engineering challenge, requiring the boring of dozens of tunnels through the Harujima mountains. The looming 1966 World Expo in Toyozawa put immense pressure on ONR to complete the line, forcing a phased opening. On May 28, 1966, the first two disconnected sections of the line opened: Okami to Hirogawa (in the north) and Toyozawa to Tsuda (in the south). A year later, on June 14, 1967, the line from Toyozawa was extended to Naruto, connecting it to the industrial hub of Anjo-Saikawa. Finally, on August 26, 1967, the massive Naruto - Hirogawa Link Bridges were completed, connecting the two sections and establishing the full Okami-Toyozawa route.

Just as the southern line was completed, national politics intervened. On June 10, 1968, widespread civil unrest and political instability, known as the [[Quiet Revolution]], caused the government to freeze all major infrastructure spending. Work on the critical northern section, connecting [[Shinagawa]] to the capital at [[Okami]], was halted for three years.

Work did not restart until August 3, 1971, under the new administration under Yoshimizu Ono. This second phase of construction also saw the groundbreaking for the [[Ishijima Shinkansen]]. The suspended Okami-Shinagawa section of the Harujima Shinkansen was finally opened on April 18, 1973, completing the nation's primary Shinkansen line.

===Expansion to the Outer Islands===
The 1970s and 1980s were defined by the expansion of the network to Okaiken's other two islands.

====Ishijima Shinkansen====

This line was built in two main, unconnected sections. Work began on the southern part (then called the ''Minatojima Shinkansen'') from Toyozawa to Minatojima on July 15, 1971, and it opened on July 19, 1975. Simultaneously, work on the northern section from Aizawa to Shin-Urayasu began on August 3, 1971, opening on May 12, 1980. The two lines operated independently for six years until the massive Tajima Link Bridges (construction started 1975) were completed on February 14, 1986, physically connecting them. On this date, the lines were formally merged to create the modern [[Ishijima Shinkansen]].

====Minami Yagikoshu Shinkansen (Green Line)====
This line's construction was a slow, multi-decade process, largely financed by the newly-privatized Okaiken Railways after the ONR's debt crisis. Work began on the first section from Toyozawa to Kagami on December 19, 1976, but it did not open until October 9, 1993. The line was extended in phases: the Kagami-Mizuno section (work began 1995) opened on April 12, 2004, and the Mizuno-Kii section (work began 2010) opened on November 9, 2018. Immediately following the 2018 opening, work began on the final extension from Kii to the northern port cities of Sakata and Aomori, which is projected to open in 2035.

